Design patterns give you a shared vocab with other developers and allows you to think at the pattern level rather
then the object level.
Communicating with patterns allows you to:
    - communicate sets of qualities, characteristics, and constraints all at the same time
    - say more with less
    - stay in design longer w/o diving into implementation
    - work quicker with less misunderstanding

Chapter One:

The Strategy Pattern: defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each one, and makes them interchangeable.
Strategy lets the algorithm vary independently from clients that use it.

- In the SimUDuck example, we defined a family of algorithms related to duck behaviors. These algorithms included
  different ways a duck could fly and quack. In order to encapsulate them, we defined two interfaces called FlyBehavior
  and QuackBehavior in which the abstract Duck class has a "HAS-A" relationship with. We used composition instead of
  inheritance to make the system more flexible to change. We also defined a subcategory of algorithms that implement
  both behaviors. Once we include the inherited setter methods in the Duck class, all subclasses of Duck could
  dynamically change their behaviors at runtime.

Chapter Two:

The Observer Pattern: defines a one-to-many dependency between objects so that when one object (subject) changes state,
all of its dependents (observers) are notified and updated automatically.
- There are numerous ways this pattern helps achieve loose coupling between objects:
    1) The only thing the subject knows about an observer is that it implements a certain interface (Observer interface)
    2) We can add new observers at any time (the subject only depends on a list of Observer implementations)
    3) We never need to modify the subject to add new types of observers (open/closed principle)
    4) We can reuse subjects and observers independently of each other
    5) Changes to the subject or the observer will not affect the other

- In the WeatherStation application, we implemented the Observer pattern by defining contracts for subjects and
  observers and implementing concrete classes with those interfaces. The goal was to abstract weather data state changes
  away from the concrete observers to support a loosely coupled system. Whenever weatherData state changed, the
  observers were notified with the update method. We later improved this implementation by using getters in concrete
  update implementations to shift from pushing to pulling. This allows observers to only get the state that they need.